---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const pageTitle = "Roof Pitch Selection Guide | Appalachian Sheds Inc.";
const pageDescription = "Compare roof slopes and understand how roof pitch affects appearance, interior volume and the character of the building.";
---
<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Building Guides</a> / 09</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">HOMEOWNER GUIDE 09 \u00b7 Size, Use &amp; Design</div><h1>Roof Pitch Selection Guide</h1><p>Compare roof slopes and understand how roof pitch affects\n              appearance, interior volume and the character of the\n              building.</p></div></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>How roof angle changes appearance, height, loft space, weather shedding, material use, and the overall character of your backyard building</p><p>Roof pitch is one of the most influential design decisions on a backyard building. It changes far more than the shape of the roof. Pitch affects overall height, interior volume, potential loft space, roof surface area, weather movement, and how naturally the finished structure relates to your home.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"roof-model-baseline\"><h2>Select roof pitch as part of the complete building</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>How roof pitch changes the building</h3><p>Roof pitch measures inches of vertical rise per 12 inches of horizontal run. A 9/12 gable has different proportions and wall-to-ridge rise than a 5/12 gable of the same span. Model architecture, building width, total height and allowable site height all matter.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>ASI approved roof profiles</h3><p>Carriage Lane: 5/12 gable. Country Cottage with Shed Dormer: 9/12 main roof. Urban Studio: 2/12 single slope. Appalachian Farmhouse: 8/12. Pine Ridge Shed: 8/12. Laurel Ridge: 2/12 mono-slope attached and stand-alone. Nordic Retreat: 10/12. Foxfire Cottage: 9/12. Grand Depot: 9/12. Appalachian Barn: gambrel upper 7/12 and lower 20/12. Appalachian Firewood Shed: 5/12. The Antique Saltbox geometry is confirmed by the final model design rather than assigned a new pitch here.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>A low-slope roof is a technical detail, not just a style</h3><p>For slopes between 2/12 and less than 4/12, ARMA identifies special underlayment approaches for asphalt shingles. Product-specific requirements and the applicable building code control the final system. A 2/12 roof cannot automatically receive ordinary steep-slope asphalt shingle detailing.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>What a steeper roof can change</h3><p>Steeper roofs can increase the building\u2019s visible height and available roof volume. However, usable loft space depends on framing, floor design, access, headroom and loads\u2014not pitch alone. More roof material and labor may affect the written proposal.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Questions to resolve before finalizing</h3><p>Confirm intended use, local height and setback limitations, roof-water management, venting, selected covering and manufacturer application limits. Ask for the model-specific design and proposed roof assembly to be confirmed in writing.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section verified-planning\" id=\"verified-planning\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">CURRENT PLANNING GUIDANCE</div><h2>Low-slope roofing requires a separate installation decision</h2><p>Roof pitch is part of a particular model\u2019s architectural identity, not a universal upgrade that can be swapped without review. The Urban Studio and both Laurel Ridge configurations have an approved 2/12 roof form; other models have different approved slopes.</p><p>ARMA describes special underlayment procedures for asphalt shingles on slopes from 2/12 to less than 4/12. The final assembly must also be permitted by the chosen shingle manufacturer and applicable code. Do not assume ordinary steep-slope shingle installation is suitable.</p><p>When comparing roof shapes, confirm the complete assembly, drainage, overall height, ventilation and engineered framing\u2014not just the appearance of the roofline.</p><p>Technical references: <a href=\"https://www.asphaltroofing.org/installation-of-asphalt-shingles-on-lower-sloped-roofs/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ARMA: asphalt shingles on lower-sloped roofs</a></p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Compare your preferred roofline with the actual model design, building height, roof-material requirements and applicable local restrictions before requesting a revised written proposal.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"references\"><h2>Relevant planning references</h2><p>These official and manufacturer resources provide additional background. Product and jurisdiction requirements must be checked for the exact project.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.asphaltroofing.org/installation-of-asphalt-shingles-on-lower-sloped-roofs/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Asphalt Roofing Manufacturers Association: low-slope installation</a></p></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#verified-planning\">Current planning guidance</a><a href=\"#roof-model-baseline\">Model-specific roof pitches</a><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a href=\"#references\">Planning references</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

<style is:global>
